From: liuqin Fedora project <liuqin@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: wcd938x-sdw: avoid RX regmap access after codec IRQ dispatch

Only the TX SoundWire peripheral creates a CSR regmap. Both RX and TX
register the implementation-defined interrupt callback and both forward
the same codec IRQ domain. An RX alert therefore dispatches the codec IRQ
and then dereferences a NULL wcd->regmap in regmap_read().

On liuqin this killed irq/259-soundwi at wcd9380_interrupt_callback+0x70
before a later suspend attempt. The SoundWire core holds sdw_dev_lock
across the callback, so the Oops also leaves that mutex locked.

Keep the nested codec IRQ dispatch for both peripherals. Return after it
when there is no local regmap; the SoundWire core can then unlock the
peripheral and acknowledge its control-port alert. Preserve the existing
TX CSR status-drain loop and all interrupt masks and PM policy.

This is a local candidate, not an upstream backport. It fixes the observed
NULL access; causality for the later suspend failure remains unproven.

Base: fe81794b5e1b8a3f11a165f3acbfdb8fa671dbb3
---
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/wcd938x-sdw.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/wcd938x-sdw.c
@@ -189,6 +189,13 @@
 
 	do {
 		handle_nested_irq(irq_find_mapping(slave_irq, 0));
+		/*
+		 * RX has no regmap, but shares the codec IRQ with TX.
+		 * The SoundWire core acknowledges the RX control-port alert.
+		 */
+		if (!wcd->regmap)
+			return IRQ_HANDLED;
+
 		regmap_read(wcd->regmap, WCD938X_DIGITAL_INTR_STATUS_0, &sts1);
 		regmap_read(wcd->regmap, WCD938X_DIGITAL_INTR_STATUS_1, &sts2);
 		regmap_read(wcd->regmap, WCD938X_DIGITAL_INTR_STATUS_2, &sts3);
